115: ## 3. V1 scope, flow, and screens
116: 
117: Ship **Builder's Workshop, Scene Machine, and Repair Café**. They train complementary skills: directing action, describing relations, and coordinating through misunderstanding. Three is enough to create contrast while keeping content and interaction review manageable. Family Resemblance Club is the first expansion candidate; no fourth game until the three pass the learning and usability gates.
118: 
119: Default path: **home → Builder's Workshop demonstration → three playable rounds → session result → suggested next game**. Experienced or returning users can choose any game immediately. Start the first demonstration in one click; no placement questionnaire or account.
120: 
121: | Route / screen | Purpose and essential UI |
122: |---|---|
123: | `/` | One primary “Start playing” button; returning “Continue”; three game cards with a plain-English skill and session duration. Small links to notebook and sources. |
124: | `/play/[game]` | Static route for each game. Scene, short goal, phrase/action tray, undo, hint, and exit. Demonstration is an interactive state here, not another onboarding page. |
125: | Round consequence | In-place state change, brief consequence text, and a repair or continue action. No score modal after every click. |
126: | Session result | Three-round results, one example of language the player used, and an optional philosophy explanation of at most 60 words with its exact reference. “Try a new situation,” “Choose a game,” and “Finish” are equally available. |
127: | `/notebook` | Locally saved completed situations, constructions practised, first-attempt versus assisted completion, and replay links. No daily calendar or streak. |
128: | `/sources` | English descriptions of the passages, distinctions between early and later work, original-text links, and a clear label for our adaptations. |
129: | `/settings` | Reduced motion, progress reset with confirmation, and plain-language privacy/storage information. Analytics choice appears only if analytics is configured. |
130: 
131: On phones, place the scene above the controls; on desktop, use scene and action panel side by side. Limit choices to four to six visible components at a time, with categories where necessary. All interactions support tap-to-select followed by tap-to-place, and keyboard equivalents. Dragging is optional. Use visible focus, announced consequence text, generous touch targets, and text or shape cues alongside colour. Every scene has a structured text equivalent that supports the same task without revealing hidden facts.
132: 
133: ## 4. Content model and production volume
134: 
135: ### Data contract, without implementation code
136: 
137: Author rounds as versioned local data. Validate it during the build. Use one shared envelope and three explicit game variants; do not invent a general scripting language.
138: 
139: | Field group | Required information and constraints |
140: |---|---|
141: | Identity | Stable round ID, content version, game ID, session ID, order, title, and guided/practice/transfer stage. IDs remain stable when wording changes. |
142: | Learning | Primary English construction, supporting vocabulary, approximate difficulty, and one observable transfer objective. |
143: | Philosophy | Work, exact section/proposition numbers, primary-source URL, editorial paraphrase, and a note describing the analogy's limits. |
144: | Presentation | Short goal, initial scene description, asset IDs, accessible object names, positions, and visibility rules. All player-facing strings are English. |
145: | World | Entities with stable IDs and discrete properties; initial state; what the player and character each know; which facts are revealed by which actions. |
146: | Language | Phrase components, grammatical combinations, semantic action ID and slots, equivalent forms, clarification prompts, and consequences. Do not compare full strings for success. |
147: | Actions | Allowed actions for this game; preconditions; deterministic state effects; resulting character line and scene change. A failed precondition has an authored explanation. |
148: | Goals and scoring | Three explicit completion predicates, accepted alternative routes, and separate first-attempt/hint metadata. Each mark is awarded at most once. |
149: | Recovery | Undoable actions, hint sequence, restart state, and an exit path from every reachable nonterminal state. |
150: | Transfer | Paired round IDs, which wording or context changes, and what behaviour would demonstrate transfer rather than memorization. |
151: | Editorial evidence | Review status, reviewer notes, accepted-route walkthroughs, and misconception checks. These are development data, not game UI. |
152: 
153: | Variant | Additional authored data |
154: |---|---|
155: | Workshop | Inventory, requested materials, quantity, destination, pointing targets, role assignment, and partner instruction mapping. |
156: | Scene Machine | Named positions, allowed relations, reference scene, editable model/report, pronoun referents, and relation predicates. Fix left/right to the viewer's perspective; define inside/behind explicitly in both layout and accessible scene text. |
157: | Repair Café | Character goal, available clues, dialogue states, information revealed, supported requests, alternative repairs, and confirmation states. |
158: 
159: **Worked record in prose:** `workshop-practice-04`, content version 1, is a directing round. Goal: finish the roof. Initial inventory includes three slabs and two pillars; the roof platform is empty. The language target is quantity plus destination. “Bring two slabs to the platform” maps to the deliver action with material slab, count two, and destination platform. Three predicates inspect delivered material, delivered count, and destination. An unpointed “that” triggers a referent question with no delivery and no score loss. Undo restores inventory and destination consistently. The paired transfer round replaces the building materials with café cups and a tray. The source record points to PI §§8, 20. Review must cover both a complete command and a clarified short command.
160: 
161: ### How much content
162: 
163: **54 rounds total: 18 per game.** Each game has six three-round sessions: one guided session, three practice sessions, and two transfer sessions. At roughly 40–70 seconds per round plus feedback, a session takes two to four minutes. Total first-pass play is roughly 36–72 minutes; this is a design estimate to test.
164: 
165: | Per-game allocation | Workshop | Scene Machine | Repair Café |
166: |---|---|---|---|
167: | 3 guided rounds | Demonstrated delivery and role reversal | Build a relation, then reveal a mismatch | Observe a misunderstanding and repair it |
168: | 9 practice rounds | Quantity, referent, destination; three each | Spatial relations, negation, conjunction; three each | Referents, requests, clarification; three each |
169: | 6 transfer rounds | Two new object sets and changed roles | New layouts and report/model direction | Same expression in changed circumstances |
170: 
171: Budget for 18 short session explanations, 54 consequence summaries, and at least two hints per round. Each café round needs at least two viable success routes and one recoverable misunderstanding: at least 36 success-route walkthroughs and 18 repair walkthroughs across that game. Workshop and scene content also require alternate valid-route examples where applicable.
172: 
173: Introduce approximately 30 useful words and 12 reusable constructions across the collection, intentionally repeated in different situations. These are content budgeting targets, not vocabulary-acquisition claims. Variants must change the language decision; palette swaps do not count as new rounds.
174: 
175: Content workflow: write a playable paper round → check the English and cited claim → enumerate accepted routes → test with a learner → encode the approved data → run reachability and scoring checks. Human editorial review precedes bulk asset production.
176: 

