177: ## 5. Art direction and gpt-image-2 image briefs
178: 
179: **Direction: an adult paper theatre.** Warm ivory paper, charcoal outlines, muted cobalt, terracotta, and sage; crisp silhouettes, restrained print texture, subtle dimensionality. Friendly and curious, without school worksheets, philosopher caricatures, or infantilizing reward graphics. Characters look like adult collaborators. Humour comes from their situations.
180: 
181: Generate with **gpt-image-2 during production**, not at runtime. No images are generated as part of this planning task. Obtain one approved reference first, then reuse it for visual consistency. Generate backgrounds separately from interactive objects so imagery never determines hit targets, text, quantities, or scoring.
182: 
183: **Shared prompt prefix:** “Create art for Words at Work, a thoughtful English-learning game for adults. Hand-cut paper theatre with subtle screen-print grain, warm ivory #F5F0E6, charcoal #252A2D, cobalt #42649A, terracotta #B96048 and sage #788A6B. Crisp, distinct silhouettes, consistent soft light from upper left, calm composition, contemporary everyday setting. No text, letters, numbers, logos, interface elements, watermarks, or embedded labels. Preserve object identity and natural proportions.”
184: 
185: Append each brief below to that prefix. Dimensions describe intended output/crop targets; inspect the available generator output and export appropriately during implementation.
186: 
187: | Image IDs and count | Target | Prompt suffix |
188: |---|---|---|
189: | `style-reference`, 1 nonshipping image | Landscape reference | “A small workshop, a tabletop theatre, and a neighbourhood café as three coordinated miniature sets; four adult collaborators and a few simple props; demonstrate one coherent material, palette and lighting treatment. A visual art-direction reference, not a user interface.” |
190: | `home-theatre`, 1 | 1600×900, with a usable central square crop | “Three connected miniature rooms: workshop left, tabletop stage centre, café right. Clear central focal point, open upper area for separately rendered heading. Inviting but quiet; no indispensable detail near the edges.” |
191: | `workshop-backdrop`, 1 | 1200×900 | “An empty workshop bench with separate left supply area and right construction platform. Front three-quarter view. Large uncluttered centre. No loose blocks, tools, characters or writing.” |
192: | `scene-backdrop`, 1 | 1200×900 | “An empty tabletop theatre with a plain wall and pale floor, shallow orthographic perspective, ample separation for movable objects. A simple open stage; no props or characters.” |
193: | `cafe-backdrop`, 1 | 1200×900 | “A small neighbourhood café counter, plain wall and neutral window opening; room for independently composited window panels, mugs and people. No printed menus, loose props or characters; strong separation between foreground counter and background.” |
194: | `builder`, `assistant`, `host`, `visitor`, 4 separate images | 768×1024, transparent background | “One full-body adult [character], relaxed attentive expression, simple readable hands, neutral three-quarter stance facing inward, no carried objects, no scenery, transparent background.” Character substitutions: builder in cobalt work jacket; assistant in sage apron; host in terracotta apron; visitor in charcoal cardigan. Vary appearance without coding competence or temperament by identity. |
195: | `block`, `pillar`, `slab`, `beam`, 4 separate images | 512×512, transparent background | “One isolated [object] made of pale stone; toy-theatre scale; transparent background; clearly different silhouette from the other building materials; no markings.” Substitutions: compact cubic block; tall cylindrical pillar; broad thin square slab; long narrow rectangular beam. |
196: | `cup`, `key`, `box`, `lamp`, 4 separate images | 512×512, transparent background | “One isolated [object], matching the paper-theatre props, front three-quarter view, transparent background, entire silhouette visible.” Substitutions: small cobalt cup; charcoal key with a large round bow; open sage box with clearly visible interior; terracotta desk lamp. |
197: | `mug`, `glass`, `menu`, `tray`, 4 separate images | 512×512, transparent background | “One isolated [object] for the café set, matching the same perspective and lighting, transparent background.” Substitutions: broad sage mug with one handle; clear drinking glass with a strong charcoal outline; closed ivory menu booklet with a blank cover; shallow terracotta serving tray. |
198: 
199: **Inventory: 21 image briefs/outputs, including one reference and 20 shipping assets.** Each named character or prop is a separate generation/output, not a sprite sheet requiring unreliable automatic slicing. Reuse props across rounds; colour and size changes are never the sole distinction needed to solve a task.
200: 
201: Produce hover, selected, and confirmation states in the interface, not additional generated pictures. Use CSS transforms for subtle movement; reduced motion removes it. Add all readable language as HTML. Export compressed backgrounds and transparent prop assets with fixed layout dimensions; inspect edges on both ivory and dark backgrounds. Initial art budget: at most 350 KB for the current backdrop and 1 MB total imagery needed by the first playable screen. Defer unrelated scenes. If a generation cannot communicate “inside” or “behind” clearly, correct the composition or use a simpler object representation before shipping.
202: 
203: ## 6. Technical architecture
204: 
205: - **Stack:** Next.js App Router, TypeScript, Tailwind, and selected shadcn/ui primitives. Use the current mutually compatible stable versions at implementation time and commit a lockfile. No additional game engine, global state framework, database, login, or runtime model API is justified by this scope. Follow the official [shadcn/ui Next.js setup](https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/installation/next).
206: - **Static delivery:** Pre-render home, sources, notebook shell, settings shell, and the three known game routes. Use static export and enumerate game parameters at build time. Export optimized image files ahead of time; the default runtime image optimizer and request-dependent server features do not fit this choice. Direct links must resolve on the eventual static host. [Next.js static export guidance](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/static-exports).
207: - **Client boundary:** Keep interactive game state and persistence in client components; browser storage is accessed only after browser initialization. Build-time content and page chrome need no interactive state. [Next.js server/client component guidance](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/server-and-client-components).
208: - **Game structure:** One shared session shell, phrase composer, consequence panel, and progress adapter; three small game-specific transition functions and renderers. Each action deterministically transforms world state, then evaluates completion predicates. Keep semantic evaluation independent of animation and image coordinates. Use ordinary React state/reducers; transitions are not timed by animation completion.
209: - **Content boundaries:** Separate game definitions, round data, source registry, asset registry, and save format. Author IDs reference registries; validation rejects dangling IDs, missing citations, duplicate IDs, unhandled actions, and rounds without reachable completion. Do not make the browser execute strings from content.
210: - **Persistence:** A versioned localStorage record contains completed round IDs, per-predicate completion, assistance metadata, last game/session, settings, and a resumable round snapshot with content version. Save after committed actions. Undo and reload must not duplicate marks. Keep progress bounded; no permanent raw interaction log. If a content revision invalidates a snapshot, preserve compatible completion and restart that round with an explanation.
211: - **Storage failure:** Catch unavailable storage, invalid JSON, quota errors, and unsupported save versions. Continue in memory with “Progress cannot be saved in this browser.” Do not overwrite an unknown newer save. A corrupt save should offer a deliberate reset rather than crash. Across tabs, merge completed IDs and keep active-round snapshots scoped per game; do not let an older tab erase newer completion. Explain that private browsing or cleared site data can remove progress and that there is no cross-device sync.
212: - **Privacy and analytics:** Default analytics off. If the owner later enables PostHog through public project configuration and the player opts in, initialize its browser SDK and send only explicit, limited learning/usability events: round completed, repair used, hint used, or a recoverable technical failure, with round/version IDs. Disable autocapture and session replay; never send phrase contents, a notebook, or full saved state. No key, opt-out, or blocked provider means zero analytics requests and fully working games. Use [PostHog's Next.js integration documentation](https://posthog.com/docs/libraries/next-js) for implementation; analytics are not required to evaluate the paper prototype.
213: - **No audio dependency:** V1 makes no pronunciation claim and requires neither microphone access nor browser speech recognition. Reading, composition and pragmatics are the promised skills. Listening or pronunciation would need a separate content and assessment design.
214: - **Offline scope:** Once a game and its assets are loaded, its round logic needs no network. Do not promise offline reopening or installability; a service worker is outside v1.
215: 
216: Suggested module boundaries for later implementation are `app`, `components/game-shell`, `games/workshop`, `games/scene-machine`, `games/repair-cafe`, `content`, and `lib/progress`. These are proposed boundaries only; this task creates no application files.
217: 
218: ## 7. Build sequence, tests, and failure gates
219: 
220: ### First and cheapest falsification test
221: 
222: Before implementation or art spending, run three paper Repair Café rounds with five intermediate learners. Give only the proposed English instructions and let a facilitator enact the authored character responses without coaching. If success depends on guessing the author's favourite sentence, or on reading the philosophy explanation first, revise the mechanic before building anything else. This test is proposed, not performed.
223: 
224: Then test one paper round each from Workshop and Scene Machine. The minimum gates are:
225: 
226: - At least four of five learners begin a meaningful action within 30 seconds without translation or coaching.
227: - At least four of five complete the paired changed-context task without copying the previous solution. They can show the distinction through action; sophisticated philosophical vocabulary is not required.
228: - Scene Machine players can handle an understandable but false report, and Café players can respond differently to identical words in changed circumstances before seeing the explanation.
229: - At least three of five say they would choose another situation when stopping has no penalty. Treat this as formative feedback, not an engagement target or proof of fun for a population.
230: - Two content reviewers, covering English pedagogy and Wittgenstein respectively, can explain the intended lesson and find no reasonable supported route incorrectly rejected. Reviewers' roles are requirements, not people already recruited.
231: 
232: ### Implementation order after approval of the concept
233: 
234: 1. Build a plain interactive slice of Repair Café: three rounds, full repair paths, tap/keyboard controls, and local save/reload. Verify its semantics before adding illustration.
235: 2. Add Workshop and Scene Machine through the same session shell. Validate that distinct mechanics fit without forcing one abstract engine onto all three.
236: 3. Author the remaining rounds in small batches, with accepted-path walkthroughs. Run learner checks on a new transfer situation before expanding each batch.
237: 4. Generate and integrate art, then finish accessibility, persistence recovery, sources, and optional analytics configuration.
238: 5. Test the production static output on phone and desktop. Publishing or deployment is a later owner decision, not part of this planning task.
239: 

