199: **Inventory: 21 image briefs/outputs, including one reference and 20 shipping assets.** Each named character or prop is a separate generation/output, not a sprite sheet requiring unreliable automatic slicing. Reuse props across rounds; colour and size changes are never the sole distinction needed to solve a task.
200: 
201: Produce hover, selected, and confirmation states in the interface, not additional generated pictures. Use CSS transforms for subtle movement; reduced motion removes it. Add all readable language as HTML. Export compressed backgrounds and transparent prop assets with fixed layout dimensions; inspect edges on both ivory and dark backgrounds. Initial art budget: at most 350 KB for the current backdrop and 1 MB total imagery needed by the first playable screen. Defer unrelated scenes. If a generation cannot communicate “inside” or “behind” clearly, correct the composition or use a simpler object representation before shipping.
202: 
203: ## 6. Technical architecture
204: 
205: - **Stack:** Next.js App Router, TypeScript, Tailwind, and selected shadcn/ui primitives. Use the current mutually compatible stable versions at implementation time and commit a lockfile. No additional game engine, global state framework, database, login, or runtime model API is justified by this scope. Follow the official [shadcn/ui Next.js setup](https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/installation/next).
206: - **Static delivery:** Pre-render home, sources, notebook shell, settings shell, and the three known game routes. Use static export and enumerate game parameters at build time. Export optimized image files ahead of time; the default runtime image optimizer and request-dependent server features do not fit this choice. Direct links must resolve on the eventual static host. [Next.js static export guidance](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/static-exports).
207: - **Client boundary:** Keep interactive game state and persistence in client components; browser storage is accessed only after browser initialization. Build-time content and page chrome need no interactive state. [Next.js server/client component guidance](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/server-and-client-components).
208: - **Game structure:** One shared session shell, phrase composer, consequence panel, and progress adapter; three small game-specific transition functions and renderers. Each action deterministically transforms world state, then evaluates completion predicates. Keep semantic evaluation independent of animation and image coordinates. Use ordinary React state/reducers; transitions are not timed by animation completion.
209: - **Content boundaries:** Separate game definitions, round data, source registry, asset registry, and save format. Author IDs reference registries; validation rejects dangling IDs, missing citations, duplicate IDs, unhandled actions, and rounds without reachable completion. Do not make the browser execute strings from content.
210: - **Persistence:** A versioned localStorage record contains completed round IDs, per-predicate completion, assistance metadata, last game/session, settings, and a resumable round snapshot with content version. Save after committed actions. Undo and reload must not duplicate marks. Keep progress bounded; no permanent raw interaction log. If a content revision invalidates a snapshot, preserve compatible completion and restart that round with an explanation.
211: - **Storage failure:** Catch unavailable storage, invalid JSON, quota errors, and unsupported save versions. Continue in memory with “Progress cannot be saved in this browser.” Do not overwrite an unknown newer save. A corrupt save should offer a deliberate reset rather than crash. Across tabs, merge completed IDs and keep active-round snapshots scoped per game; do not let an older tab erase newer completion. Explain that private browsing or cleared site data can remove progress and that there is no cross-device sync.
212: - **Privacy and analytics:** Default analytics off. If the owner later enables PostHog through public project configuration and the player opts in, initialize its browser SDK and send only explicit, limited learning/usability events: round completed, repair used, hint used, or a recoverable technical failure, with round/version IDs. Disable autocapture and session replay; never send phrase contents, a notebook, or full saved state. No key, opt-out, or blocked provider means zero analytics requests and fully working games. Use [PostHog's Next.js integration documentation](https://posthog.com/docs/libraries/next-js) for implementation; analytics are not required to evaluate the paper prototype.
213: - **No audio dependency:** V1 makes no pronunciation claim and requires neither microphone access nor browser speech recognition. Reading, composition and pragmatics are the promised skills. Listening or pronunciation would need a separate content and assessment design.
214: - **Offline scope:** Once a game and its assets are loaded, its round logic needs no network. Do not promise offline reopening or installability; a service worker is outside v1.
215: 
216: Suggested module boundaries for later implementation are `app`, `components/game-shell`, `games/workshop`, `games/scene-machine`, `games/repair-cafe`, `content`, and `lib/progress`. These are proposed boundaries only; this task creates no application files.
240: ### Required evidence for a future release
241: 
242: | Area | Real check and success condition |
243: |---|---|
244: | Content and scoring | Validate all 54 records; execute every authored success and repair path; check reachability, equivalent utterances, valid alternative layouts, and score idempotence. |
245: | Actual play | Complete one three-round session per game from the production output using touch, then keyboard; goals and reactions remain legible without developer knowledge. |
246: | Learning | Repeat the small formative learner test using unfamiliar transfer rounds. Record observed actions separately from opinions and explanation recall. A small sample does not establish long-term efficacy. |
247: | Persistence | Reload mid-round, close/reopen, switch tabs, and reset. Simulate corrupt, blocked, full, old-version and newer-version storage; there is no crash or silent loss of compatible completion. |
248: | Accessibility | Check 360-pixel phone width, 200% zoom, keyboard focus, a screen reader, reduced motion, and non-colour cues. Complete the same goals without dragging or relying on decorative art. |
249: | Delivery | Serve the exported production build and open every route directly, including invalid game URLs. No development server behaviour substitutes for static-host verification. |
250: | Performance | On a representative midrange phone with throttled network, target first playable interaction within 3 seconds and immediate action feedback within 100 ms; record device and conditions. Asset budgets are hypotheses until measured. |
251: | Analytics | Inspect actual network requests with configuration absent, opt-out, and opt-in. First two produce no PostHog calls; opt-in sends only approved explicit events. |
252: | Editorial accuracy | Confirm each published explanation against its cited passage, distinguish analogy from textual claim, and verify the chosen translation before adding any direct quotations. |
253: 
254: ## 8. Risks and conditions that would make this fail
255: 
256: | Failure | Early signal | Response |
257: |---|---|---|
258: | Disguised quizzes | The player selects a sentence and receives correctness feedback without any useful world change. | Require action consequences and a repair path; cut rounds whose English is ornamental. |
259: | Learning exists only in the debrief | Players repeat the same mistake in the changed situation until the explanation tells them what to do. | Redesign the contrast and transfer round; do not lengthen the lecture. |
260: | Misleading philosophy | “All meaning is use,” “false means meaningless,” or “anything follows a rule” appears in feedback. | Keep interpretations qualified; enforce passage review and remove overclaims. |
261: | Arbitrary pragmatics | A sensible request fails because the author secretly preferred another wording. | Add the valid route, supply missing context, or narrow the task honestly. Never use nationality as an intention shortcut. |
262: | Guessing through components | Learners succeed by trying every highlighted phrase without understanding its effect. | Use visible consequences and novel transfer tasks; remove answer-revealing highlights after guidance. |
263: | Difficulty mismatch | Beginners cannot parse instructions or intermediate learners face only noun matching. | Test B1–B2 readers; simplify instructions while increasing contextual decisions and role reversals. |
264: | Content dominates cost | Every new round requires new mechanics, art, or a large bespoke dialogue tree. | Reuse the three bounded systems and assets; prefer fewer distinctive contexts to superficial variants. |
265: | Art undermines play | Objects overlap, gestures reveal answers, or “inside” is visually ambiguous. | Make state explicit in layout and accessible descriptions; revise art before expanding it. |
266: | Local progress feels unreliable | Refresh loses a round, updates erase completion, or another tab overwrites progress. | Treat recovery and cross-tab behaviour as release requirements, not later polish. |
267: | Scope drifts into a platform | Accounts, AI conversation, pronunciation scoring, and social features displace good rounds. | Keep them outside v1; do not weaken the three-game evidence gates to fund breadth. |
268: 
269: ## 9. Evidence and boundaries of this plan
270: 
271: Read and used: `planning/BRIEF.md`; the linked primary texts of *Philosophical Investigations*, *Tractatus*, and *On Certainty*; and the linked official Next.js, shadcn/ui, and PostHog documentation. Philosophical explanations here are paraphrases, not quotations from an English translation. The game mechanics are interpretive teaching proposals and still require editorial and learner review.
272: 
273: The required local-source check consulted the [Jackson wiki APEX results-copy note](C:/Users/Administrator/Documents/projects/LLMwiki/APEX/APEX-SUT-Results-Copy-Mechanism.md). It concerns laboratory test-result transfer and contributes no evidence to this product plan; a wiki search found no Wittgenstein or language-learning material to use.
274: 
275: **Also done:** included transfer criteria, multiple-valid-answer scoring, and local-storage recovery requirements so the implementation can be judged beyond a successful build. These additions are confined to this document and can be undone by removing their corresponding planning text.
276: 
277: **Not covered:** no application, generated art, prototype, deployment, learner session, or learning outcome has been produced or tested. The present deliverable is the independent Markdown plan only.

