94: ### 1.2 Go On! (teach the pupil). v1
95: 
96: - **Idea.**
97:   - PI §143 and §151: teaching a pupil a series; "Now I can go on!".
98:   - §185: a pupil trained on "+2" writes 1000, 1004, 1008, 1012 and is sure he has gone on in the same way.
99:     Every example he was shown fits his reading too.
100:   - §198: an interpretation "hangs in the air" along with what it interprets; we are trained to react to a
101:     signpost.
102:   - §201: there is a way of grasping a rule that is not an interpretation.
103:   - §199 and §202: obeying a rule is a custom, a practice, and cannot be done "privately".
104:   - §206, §217, §219: when the justifications run out, "This is simply what I do"; I obey the rule blindly.
105:   - §241: agreement in form of life.
106: - **English target: morphology and spelling, B1-B2.** These are rules whose examples do not settle them:
107:   - *a/an* chosen by the first sound (*an hour, a university, an MBA*).
108:   - Plural spelling (*-s, -es, -ies, -ves*) and irregular plurals.
109:   - Past-tense *-ed* spelling, including consonant doubling (*stopped, visited, preferred*).
110:   - *-ed* pronunciation (/t/, /d/, /ɪd/).
111:   - Comparatives (*bigger, happier, more careful*).
112:   - Uncountable nouns (*advice, information, furniture*).
113:   - Verb + *-ing* versus verb + *to* (*enjoy swimming, want to swim*).
114: - **Mechanic.** You are the teacher.
115:   - The pupil holds several *ways of going on*: the correct one plus deviant ones such as "*an* before a vowel
116:     *letter*" or "always *-s*". It uses its favourite way among those that fit every card it has seen.
117:   - You show example cards from a deck. After each card the pupil re-sits a 5-item exam, and you watch its
118:     answers change.
119:   - When the whole exam is right, the pupil says "Now I can go on!". Par is the fewest cards that rule out every
120:     deviant way, computed from the data.
121:   - You can also play an **Explain** card, a rule in words such as "Use *an* before a vowel". The pupil reads it
122:     its own way (vowel *letter*), because an explanation is one more sign to interpret (§198). An Explain card
123:     costs the same as an example card.
124:   - **The "1000" moment** comes next: one item beyond the training range. Sometimes the pupil goes wrong again,
125:     and sometimes English speakers themselves differ. You make the teacher's call: *correct it*, or *both are in
126:     use*.
127:   - The debrief: no finite list of examples, and no rule statement, settles the next step by itself. Training
128:     into a shared practice does (§§198-202, §241).
129: - **Round example (Level 1, "A or an?").**
130:   - Exam: *hour, university, apple, MBA, horse*. Ways: *always a*, *letter*, *sound* (correct). Deck: *egg,
131:     umbrella, unicorn, hotel, cat*.
132:   - The pupil starts with *always a* and scores 2/5 ("a hour", "a apple", …).
133:   - Show *an egg*: *always a* is ruled out. The pupil switches to *letter* ("a hour", "an university", "a MBA")
134:     and still scores 2/5.
135:   - Show *a unicorn*: *letter* is ruled out. The pupil scores 5/5: "Now I can go on!". Par is 2.
136:   - Twist: *URL*. Most people say "you-are-el" and write *a URL*; some say "earl" and write *an URL*. The right
137:     call is *both are in use*, because the practice of speakers decides.
138: - **Why it's fun.** It is reverse Zendo: you hold the secret rule. The pupil's earnest mistakes (*an university,
139:   childs, informations*) are the comedy, and they are the learner's own past errors. Finding the one card that
140:   flips the pupil is a satisfying puzzle, and you have to really know a rule to teach it.
141: - **Scoring (0-3).**
142:   - 1 mark: the pupil passes the exam. Required to finish the round.
143:   - +1: no more cards than par.
144:   - +1: the right call at the "1000" moment. This is a judgement about English usage (what speakers do), never a
145:     question about the philosophy.
146: - **Trap.** The game must not end in scepticism ("there is no fact about meaning", Kripke's reading). The
147:   debrief lands on Wittgenstein's own answer: following a rule is a practice we are trained into (§§198-202),
148:   and justification ends in what we do (§217).
149: 
150: ### 1.3 Family Likeness (find the threads). v1
151: 
152: - **Idea.**
153:   - PI §65-66: what is common to all games: board games, card games, ball games, Olympic games? "Don't think,
154:     but look!": similarities crop up and disappear.
155:   - §66 supplies its own counterexamples: patience (no competition between players), a child throwing a ball at
156:     a wall and catching it (no winning and losing), ring-a-ring-a-roses (amusement, but skill and luck are
157:     gone). §66 also asks whether all games are "amusing", comparing chess with noughts and crosses.
158:   - §67: *family resemblances*. A thread's strength lies in many overlapping fibres, not in one fibre running
159:     its whole length. Calling "the disjunction of all their common properties" a common feature is only playing
160:     with words.
161:   - §69: we explain "game" by examples: "this and similar things". We can draw a boundary for a special purpose,
162:     as when 1 pace is defined as 75 cm.
163:   - §71: blurred edges; "Stand roughly there."
164:   - §88: "inexact" does not mean "unusable".
165:   - BB Ts-309, 27-29: the "craving for generality" and the "contemptuous attitude towards the particular case".
166: - **English target: grammar and pragmatics, B1-B2.** Generalising honestly:
167:   - Quantifiers that match the evidence (*all, most, some, no, not all*).
168:   - Scope of negation: *Not all games have a winner* versus the ambiguous *All games don't have a winner*.
169:   - Subject-verb agreement in general statements (*A game has / Games have*).
170:   - Comparison (*both … and, neither … nor, unlike, whereas*).
171:   - Hedging (*roughly, tend to*).
172:   - This feeds directly into IELTS and TOEFL writing, where over-general claims lose marks.
173: - **Mechanic.** Each concept (*game, tool, sport, chair, food, book, home, music*) has 6 picture cards of things
174:   we call by that word, and 6-8 feature tiles. There are three round types, about 60 seconds each.
175:   - *Claim* (§66): make the strongest true general claim.
176:     - You build *All games have a winner* and press **Look**.
177:     - Cards with the feature light up and the rest stay dark. The Objector holds up a dark one: "Think of a
178:       child throwing a ball at a wall and catching it. Who wins?"
179:     - You fix the claim: *Most games have a winner*, or *Not all games have a winner*.
180:     - Truth is computed from the cards, so the right quantifier is a fact about what you can see.
181:   - *Thread* (§67): link the six cards into one rope.
182:     - Each link is a comparison sentence built from tiles, such as *Patience and poker are both played with
183:       cards*.
184:     - Each true link adds a coloured fibre. The finished rope shows overlapping colours, and no colour runs from
185:       end to end.
186:   - *Edge* (§69, §71): a new card arrives, such as a crossword, a video game or a lottery.
187:     - Link it if you can make a true link, or leave it at the edge. Both are accepted ("Stand roughly there",
188:       §71).
189:     - In *special-purpose* rounds you are given a purpose, such as "entries for a two-player tournament". You
190:       then draw a sharp boundary with one *must* rule, which is right for that purpose only (§69).
191:   - Cards where a feature is genuinely unclear are marked *blurred* in the data (is chess "amusing"? §66 asks).
192:     Blurred cards glow half-lit and count neither for nor against a claim.
193: - **Round example (Claim, concept *game*).**
194:   - Cards: chess, a football match, poker, patience, a child throwing a ball at a wall, ring-a-ring-a-roses.
195:   - Draft 1: *All games have a winner*. The wall card and ring-a-ring-a-roses stay dark, and the Objector plays
196:     the wall card (§66).
197:   - Draft 2: *All games don't have a winner*. Note: "This can mean *no game has a winner*. To say that some
198:     games lack one, write *Not all games have a winner*."
199:   - Draft 3: *Most games have a winner*. This is true (4 of 6) and the most informative claim: 3 marks. The
200:     debrief names §66.
201: - **Why it's fun.** The Objector's counterexample is a small comic jab, the light-up cards make "don't think, but
202:   look" literal, and the finished rope is a satisfying visual payoff. Rounds are short, and each round type
203:   plays differently.
204: - **Scoring (0-3).**
205:   - *Claim*: 1 mark for a true claim; +1 for the most informative true quantifier (*most* at 4/6, not *some*);
206:     +1 for no English notes.
207:   - *Thread*: 1 mark when all cards are linked by true sentences; +1 for at least 3 different fibres; +1 for no
208:     notes.
209:   - *Edge*: 1 mark for a placement backed by a true sentence; +1 when the special-purpose rule sorts the entries
210:     correctly (or, with no purpose given, when a borderline card is honestly left at the edge); +1 for no notes.
211: - **Trap.** The game must not claim more than §66 does.
212:   - The feature lists are chosen so that no listed feature runs through all six cards; a content test enforces
213:     this. The debrief says "none of *these* features", not "games have nothing in common".
214:   - Wittgenstein's own examples (patience, the wall, ring-a-ring-a-roses, chess) carry a §66 chip only for the
215:     feature he used them for. All other counterexamples are ours.
216: 

