173: - **Mechanic.** Each concept (*game, tool, sport, chair, food, book, home, music*) has 6 picture cards of things
174:   we call by that word, and 6-8 feature tiles. There are three round types, about 60 seconds each.
175:   - *Claim* (§66): make the strongest true general claim.
176:     - You build *All games have a winner* and press **Look**.
177:     - Cards with the feature light up and the rest stay dark. The Objector holds up a dark one: "Think of a
178:       child throwing a ball at a wall and catching it. Who wins?"
179:     - You fix the claim: *Most games have a winner*, or *Not all games have a winner*.
180:     - Truth is computed from the cards, so the right quantifier is a fact about what you can see.
181:   - *Thread* (§67): link the six cards into one rope.
182:     - Each link is a comparison sentence built from tiles, such as *Patience and poker are both played with
183:       cards*.
184:     - Each true link adds a coloured fibre. The finished rope shows overlapping colours, and no colour runs from
185:       end to end.
186:   - *Edge* (§69, §71): a new card arrives, such as a crossword, a video game or a lottery.
187:     - Link it if you can make a true link, or leave it at the edge. Both are accepted ("Stand roughly there",
188:       §71).
189:     - In *special-purpose* rounds you are given a purpose, such as "entries for a two-player tournament". You
190:       then draw a sharp boundary with one *must* rule, which is right for that purpose only (§69).
191:   - Cards where a feature is genuinely unclear are marked *blurred* in the data (is chess "amusing"? §66 asks).
192:     Blurred cards glow half-lit. A claim counts as true only if it holds however the blurred cards are counted.
193:     If the result depends on them, the claim is *open*. It scores nothing, but it is not marked wrong: the
194:     Objector holds up the blurred card and asks whether it counts (§3.3).
195: - **Round example (Claim, concept *game*).**
196:   - Cards: chess, a football match, poker, patience, a child throwing a ball at a wall, ring-a-ring-a-roses.
197:   - Draft 1: *All games have a winner*. The wall card and ring-a-ring-a-roses stay dark, and the Objector plays
198:     the wall card (§66).
199:   - Draft 2: *All games don't have a winner*. Note: "This can mean *no game has a winner*. To say that some
200:     games lack one, write *Not all games have a winner*."
201:   - Draft 3: *Most games have a winner*. This is true (4 of 6) and the most informative claim: 3 marks. The
202:     debrief names §66.
203: - **Why it's fun.** The Objector's counterexample is a small comic jab, the light-up cards make "don't think, but
204:   look" literal, and the finished rope is a satisfying visual payoff. Rounds are short, and each round type
205:   plays differently.
206: - **Scoring (0-3).**
207:   - *Claim*: 1 mark for a true claim; +1 for the most informative true quantifier (*most* at 4/6, not *some*);
208:     +1 for no English notes.
209:   - *Thread*: 1 mark when all cards are linked by true sentences; +1 for at least 3 different fibres; +1 for no
210:     notes.
211:   - *Edge*: 1 mark for a placement backed by a true sentence; +1 when the special-purpose rule sorts the entries
212:     correctly (or, with no purpose given, when a borderline card is honestly left at the edge); +1 for no notes.
213: - **Trap.** The game must not claim more than §66 does.
214:   - The feature lists are chosen so that no listed feature runs through all six cards; a content test enforces
215:     this. The debrief says "none of *these* features", not "games have nothing in common".
216:   - Wittgenstein's own examples (patience, the wall, ring-a-ring-a-roses, chess) carry a §66 chip only for the
217:     feature he used them for. All other counterexamples are ours.
218: 
671: What the data implies:
672: 
673: - *Winner* is true for 4 of 6 cards, so the best claim is *most*. *Skill* is 5/6, also *most*. *Compete*, *luck*,
674:   *cards*, *ball* and *alone* each come to *some* and *not all*. *Luck* at 3/6 is exactly half, so *most* is
675:   false; the note says "3 of 6 is half, not most".
676: - *Amuse* is definite for 3 cards and blurred for 3. Only *some* is safe; *all*, *most* and *not all* stay open.
677:   This is §66's own question, whether all games are amusing, and the game leaves it open as §66 does.
678: - No feature is shared by all six cards.
679: - A thread exists through all six cards: football–chess (*compete*) → chess–poker (*winner*) → poker–patience
680:   (*cards*) → patience–wall (*alone*) → wall–ring (*amuse*).
681: 
682: How claims are judged:
683: 
684: - Let *kMin* be the number of cards that definitely have the feature, *kMax* = kMin + the blurred cards, and *n* = 6.
685: - A claim is **true** if it holds at both kMin and kMax, **false** if it fails at both, and **open** otherwise.
686:   Every quantifier below is monotone in k, so checking the two ends is enough.
687: 
688: | Quantifier | True at k when | Earns the "most informative" mark when the claim is true and (k = kMin) |
689: |---|---|---|
690: | *all* | k = n | always |
691: | *most* | k > n/2 | k < n |
692: | *some* | k ≥ 1 | k ≤ n/2, or the claim is open for every stronger quantifier |
693: | *not all* | k < n | k ≤ n/2 |
694: | *no* | k = 0 | always |
